= Graph Theory

#statement("Theorem")[
For every group $G$, there is a connected two dimensional CW complex $X$ with $pi_1\(X\)= G$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
We can always find a surjection from a free group $F$ to $G$, suppose $F$ is generated by $g_alpha$'s, and the kernel $K$ is generated by $r_beta$'s, i.e. $F$ has a group presentation $chevron.l g_alpha\|r_beta chevron.r$, then define $X$ to be $or.big_alpha S_alpha^1$ attached with cells $e_beta^2$'s along each word $r_beta$

]
#statement("Definition")[
Cayley graphs, Cayley complexes

]
#statement("Definition")[
A #strong[graph]#index-entry("Graph") $G$ is a one dimensional CW complex, a #strong[tree]#index-entry("Tree") $T$ is a contractible graph, $T subset.eq G$ is maximal if $T$ contains all vertices, note that in a tree there is a unique path between two vertices

]
#statement("Proposition")[
Let $X$ be a connected graph, any tree in $X$ is contained in a maximal tree, in particular, $X$ has a maximal tree

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Let's prove more generally any subgraph $X_0$ is the deformation retraction of subgraph $Y$ which contains all the vertices

Construct $X_0 subset.eq X_1 subset.eq X_2 subset.eq dots.h.c$ as follows, $X_(i + 1)$ is obtained by adding the closures of all the edges that connected to $X_i$, $X = union.big_i X_i$, since $X$ is path connected, let $Y_0 = X_0$, and construct $Y_0 subset.eq Y_1 subset.eq Y_2 subset.eq dots.h.c$ as follows, for any vertex in $X_(i + 1) - X_i$, choose one edge that connects to $Y_i$, and add the closure, so we have $Y_(i + 1)$ from $Y_i$, it is easy to see the $Y_(i + 1)$ deformation retracts onto $Y_i$, so $Y = union.big_i Y_i$ deformation retracts onto $Y_0 = X_0$

If $X_0 = T$ is a tree, so is $Y$ since $Y$ deformation retracts onto $T$ which is contractible

]
#statement("Proposition", title: [Free basis for connected graphs])[
For a connected graph $X$ with maximal tree $T$, for any edge $e_alpha in X - T$, there is a corresponding loop $f_alpha$ goes from $x_0$ to one endpoint of $e_alpha$, across $e_alpha$ to the other, and go back to $x_0$, $pi_1\(X\,x_0\)$ is a free group generated by $f_alpha$

]#label("Free basis for connected graphs")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Consider $X arrow.r X\/T$ which is a homotopy equivalence

]
#statement("Theorem")[
Any subgroup of a free group is also free

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Let $F$ be a free group, there exists a graph $X$ such that $pi_1\(X\)= F$ by just taking the wedge sum of circles at $x_0$, let $G lt.eq F$ be a subgroup, then there exists a covering $Y arrow.r^p X$ such that $p_(*)\(pi_1\(Y\,y_0\)\)= G$, thus $pi_1\(Y\,y_0\)tilde.equiv G$, and since $Y$ is a covering of $X$, $Y$ is also a graph, by Proposition #ref(label("Free basis for connected graphs"), supplement: none), $G tilde.equiv pi_1\(Y\)$ is free

]
#statement("Definition")[
A #strong[complete graph]#index-entry("Complete graph") $K_n$ consists of $n$ vertices and all possible edges

#figure([#universe-drawing(17.0)],
  caption: [
    $K_5$
  ]
)
#label("K5 complete graph")

#strong[Complete bipartite]#index-entry("Complete bipartite") graphs are $K_(n\,m)$ with $n\,m$ vertices on each side and all possible edges between them

#figure([#universe-drawing(18.0)],
  caption: [
    $K_(2\,4)$
  ]
)
#label("K2,4 complete bipartite graph")

]
#statement("Definition")[
$G$ is $k$ vertex connected if $G$ has more than $k$ vertices and remain connected when removing less than $k$ vertices

]
#statement("Proposition")[
Every convex polytope can be represented by a $3$ vertex connected planar graph

]
#aside("Remark")[
A graph embedded in the plane through different ways may have different dual graphs

#figure([#box(image("Pictures/Different_dual_graphs.png", width: 65.0%))],
  caption: [
    Different dual graphs for different planar embeddings of the same graph
  ]
)
#label("Different dual graphs")

However, if the graph is $3$ vertex connected, then the dual graph will be canonical

]
